      "output": {
        "message": "Living together makes this trickier because you don't have natural physical distance to buffer the questions. To build more privacy in the house, a few practical shifts help: 1. **Set the boundary outside the moment:** Bring it up when nobody is actively asking, so it doesn't feel defensive. For example: *“Since we share a house, my dating life is one thing I really want to keep private. If there’s ever someone serious, I promise I’ll let you know, but otherwise, I’d prefer we don’t talk about it.”*\n2. **Use a flat, broken-record reply:** When it does get brought up, don't give new details to latch onto. Stick to a short, repeatable line like, *“Nothing new to share on that,”* and immediately pivot to a household topic like dinner or chores.\n3. **Keep your plans low-detail:** Avoid offering information that invites questions. If you're heading out, a simple *“I’m going out for a couple of hours”* or *“Meeting a friend for food”* leaves less room for them to pry.",
